I support our team's decision to use a Decision Tree instead of a simple RMS threshold to classify neck muscle activity. Our model achieved 87.7% mean accuracy during leave-one-session-out cross-validation, using six EMG features. Although logistic regression achieved a similar accuracy of 86.8%, we preferred the Decision Tree because its rules are easier to interpret. The model mainly used Waveform Length (WL) and Median Frequency (MDF), suggesting that amplitude alone may not capture all relevant signal differences. Phinyomark et al. (2012), *Feature Reduction and Selection for EMG Signal Classification*, explain how selecting informative EMG features supports classification performance.
